      "documentConcept": "Disease-modification trial landscape and lessons from failed trials",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:d0fd6fffeca7a4855be8b188eab130de98b3b085ce88d6f10fd07badf14507e8",
      "text": "Recent reviews state that no disease-modifying therapy for Parkinson's disease has been established. This concept collects registry and journal results from trials that did not show disease modification (SURE-PD3 inosine, STEADY-PD III isradipine, FAIRPARK-II deferiprone, SPARK cinpanemab) and, separately, the reasons reviewers give for such failures: symptomatic effects confound disease-modification readouts, delayed-start interpretation is contested, target engagement rarely coincided with clinical benefit in one review, and authors cite patient variability, late-stage pathology and insensitive endpoints. It also notes a post hoc re-analysis that disputes a clean negative reading of isradipine and a proposed multi-arm, multi-stage platform trial.",
